body:
Checkpointing serializes the component tree and POSTs it to an API so that users can visualize and debug their workflows including component props and outputs. We need to provide a way to mask sensitive information, such as `OPENAI_API_KEY`, so that it is redacted from the checkpoint.

## Proposal

I suggest we take a simple approach to this where we add a `ComponentOpts` type that can be specified (1) at the time a component is authored via an additional parameter after `componentName` to set defaults and (2) when components are declared via an additional prop. 

Extending the `Component` interface:

```tsx
type ComponentOpts = {
  secrets?: string[];  // Props to mask in checkpoints
  // Future extensibility built in:
  retry?: RetryConfig;
  timeout?: number;
  // etc...
}

function Component<P, O>(
  name: string,
  fn: (props: P) => MaybePromise<O>,
  opts?: ComponentOpts
): GsxComponent<P, O>

Extending props:

```tsx
// Base ComponentOpts type
type ComponentOpts = {
  secrets?: string[];
  // ... other component-level options
}

// Helper type to inject componentOpts into props
type WithComponentOpts<P> = P & {
  componentOpts?: ComponentOpts;
}

// Update the GsxComponent type to use the enhanced props
type GsxComponent<P, O> = {
  (props: WithComponentOpts<P>): Promise<O>;
  // ... other existing properties
}

// Update the Component function signature
function Component<P, O>(
  name: string,
  fn: (props: P) => MaybePromise<O>,
  defaultOpts?: ComponentOpts
): GsxComponent<WithComponentOpts<P>, O>
```

Example usage: 

```tsx
const OpenAIProvider = Component<{ apiKey: string }, void>(
  "OpenAIProvider",
  ({ apiKey }) => { ... },
  { secrets: ['apiKey'] }
);

// Usage - TypeScript knows componentOpts is available
<OpenAIProvider 
  apiKey={process.env.OPENAI_API_KEY}
  componentOpts={{ 
    secrets: ['apiKey', 'otherSecret'] // Extend/override defaults
  }} 
/>
```

## Open questions

- Does masking need to be transitive, ie if a prop is fed into another component prop or output, should those values be masked as well?

comments.body:
This looks very reasonable to me. Only thing to consider is if we want to put ops right after the name. I.e. 

``` typescript
const OpenAIProvider = Component<{ apiKey: string }, void>(
  "OpenAIProvider",
  { secrets: ['apiKey'] },
  ({ apiKey }) => { ... }
);
/>
```

Looks great. One thought would be to support a way to support extending the existing values without needing to know them:

```
type ComponentOpts = {
  secrets?: string[] | (existingSecretsMask: string[]) => string[] // A function lets you keep the see the existing values, and return whatever is appropriate.
}
```

body:
Currently, it can be tricky to handle scenarios where either a) you need to handle null items in a list or b) you need to flatten a list of lists. This is because the gensx elements haven't been resolved by the time you're running `.filter()` or `.flat()`.

Take the following example:

```typescript
<PromptToQuery prompt={prompt}>
        {({ queries }) => {
          return queries.map((query) => (
            <ArxivSearch query={query}>
              {(documents: ArxivEntry[]) => {
                console.log(`\n=== Results for query: "${query}" ===`);
                return documents.map((document) => (
                  <DocumentGrader prompt={prompt} document={document}>
                    {(gradeResult: DocumentGraderOutput) => {
                      console.log(
                        `Title: ${document.title}\nUseful: ${gradeResult.useful}\n`
                      );
                      return gradeResult.useful ? document : null;
                    }}
                  </DocumentGrader>
                )).filter((doc): doc is ArxivEntry => doc !== null); // <- this filter does not work
              }}
            </ArxivSearch>
          ));
        }}
</PromptToQuery>
```

The filter throws an error saying `A type predicate's type must be assignable to its parameter's type.Type 'ArxivEntry' is not assignable to type 'Element'.`

We should define a pattern for making it easy to filter and flatten list or, at a minimum, provide prescriptive guidance to users on how to handle this.

comments.body:
One option is to add a set of gsx equivelents to `array.map` `array.filter`, etc. 

Roughly, these would just be syntactic sugar over `gsx.execute`:

```tsx
function map(items Element[], mapper: (item: any) => any){
 return await gsx.execute(items).map(mapper)
}

function filter(items Element[], filterFn: (item: any) => any){
 return await gsx.execute(items).filter(filterFn)
}
```

Need to get more concrete on the semantics, what it accepts/doesn't accept, and how the calls can be composed/chained together since they do produce promises.

What about something like this? 

```tsx
interface ProviderComponentConfig<P, C> {
  // Hook to transform/wrap children before execution
  transformChildren: (props: P, children: JSX.Element) => Promise<JSX.Element>;
  // Optional setup/cleanup hooks
  setup?: (props: P) => Promise<C>;
  cleanup?: () => Promise<void>;
}

const OpenAIProvider = gsx.ProviderComponent<ClientOptions, OpenAI>({
  setup: async (props) => new OpenAI(props),
  transformChildren: async (props, children) => (
    <OpenAIContext.Provider value={{ client: await setup(props) }}>
      {children}
    </OpenAIContext.Provider>
  )
});
```

I think the problem right now is that the `OpenAIProvider` that we export is itself a `gsx.Component` so of course the double execution happens. 

If we create a new concept `gsx.ProviderComponent` - then provider component could just have special casing. It doesn't get executed or produce outputs, it just transforms its children.

After poking at it some more, I was able to land on an implementation that supports this pattern:

```
export const OpenAIProvider = gsx.Component<ClientOptions, never>((props) => {
  const client = new OpenAI(rest);
  return (
    <OpenAIContext.Provider value={{ client }}/>
  );
});
```

This means that we do not need to immediately support exposing `props.children`, and we do not need to worry about duplicate execution of children. It did require special handling of `ExecutionContext` return values in `jsx()`, but that seems like an ok way to handle this.

